Package: pad-osk
Version: 1.0
Architecture: arm64
Maintainer: erhai <user@opd2413.local>
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Depends: gnome-shell (>= 45), gnome-shell (<< 60), libglib2.0-bin
Recommends: ibus, ibus-libpinyin
Description: On-screen keyboard with palettes, sizes and transparency
 A GNOME Shell extension that draws a PC-style on-screen keyboard and injects
 keys through Clutter.VirtualInputDevice, inside the compositor.
 .
 This is the only approach that works on GNOME Wayland: normal windows cannot
 accept a click without stealing keyboard focus, and mutter refuses external
 input injection (no zwp_virtual_keyboard, no wlr-layer-shell). Because the keys
 are injected as real input events, apps that never speak zwp_text_input_v3 --
 Chromium and Electron (Chrome, QQ) -- receive them normally.
 .
 Layout is PC-style with a number row, sticky modifiers, arrow keys and a
 Chinese/English input-method toggle. Every character key, the erase key and
 the arrows repeat while held. A settings sheet behind the grip bar's gear
 button picks the key size (small / medium / large, 55% / 66% / 76% of the
 screen width), the palette (graphite / light / warm / high contrast, plus a
 user-defined palette read from the [custom] group), how see-through the whole
 keyboard is (100% down to 50% -- panel, keys and glyphs fade together) and an
 optional background image from ~/.config/pad-osk/backgrounds/. A reset
 button returns everything to the defaults. Settings are remembered per user
 and can be fine-tuned from ~/.config/pad-osk/position.kf. The panel can be
 dragged, collapsed to a thin bar, closed, and hidden from Quick Settings or
 the application grid. It steps aside when the shutdown/session dialog
 appears.
